(vector (map (:adr/authoritative_for (vector "kotoba-lang/dtn の TURN allocation/permission 自動更新（schedule-turn-refresh!）の設計根拠")), (:adr/supersedes (vector)), (:adr/superseded_by (vector)), (:adr/topic "telecom-independent-substrate-dtn-rcs-mesh-satellite"), (:adr/related (vector "90-docs/adr/2607162304-kotoba-lang-dtn-udp-turn-nat-traversal.edn" "orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn" "orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:adr/id "adr-2607162331-kotoba-lang-dtn-turn-allocation-refresh"), (:adr/doc_type :adr), (:adr/last_verified "2026-07-16"), (:adr/body "---\nid: adr-2607162331-kotoba-lang-dtn-turn-allocation-refresh\ntitle: \"ADR-2607162331: kotoba-lang/dtn gains automatic TURN allocation/permission refresh — a :turn-relay node stays reachable indefinitely instead of silently expiring\"\nstatus: accepted\ndoc_type: adr\ntopic: telecom-independent-substrate-dtn-rcs-mesh-satellite\nauthoritative: true\nlast_verified: 2026-07-16\nauthoritative_for:\n  - kotoba-lang/dtn の TURN allocation/permission 自動更新（schedule-turn-refresh!）の設計根拠\nrelated:\n  - 90-docs/adr/2607162304-kotoba-lang-dtn-udp-turn-nat-traversal.edn\n  - orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn\n  - orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn\nsupersedes: []\nsuperseded_by: []\n---\n\n# ADR-2607162331: kotoba-lang/dtn gains automatic TURN allocation/permission refresh — a :turn-relay node stays reachable indefinitely instead of silently expiring\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-16\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki\n\n## Problem\n\nADR-2607162304's own Consequences section named, as an explicitly disclosed\nscope reduction, that `kotoba.dtn.transport.udp`'s `:turn-relay` NAT\ntraversal made only a single Allocate/CreatePermission call at startup —\n\"a relay allocated at startup will silently expire after TURN's default\n~5-minute lifetime — a real limitation for any long-running deployment.\"\nHaving just closed the whole ADR series' originating gap and declared its\n\"実際に稼働可能\" (actually-operational) claim, this specific limitation\ndirectly undercut that claim for any real, non-trivially-short-lived usage.\nThis ADR closes it.\n\n## Decision\n\n### Decision 1: periodic refresh, cadence derived from the server's actual grant, not a hardcoded assumption\n\n`schedule-turn-refresh!` reads the LIFETIME actually returned by the\noriginal Allocate response (`granted-lifetime-s`) rather than assuming\nRFC 8656's 600s default holds — refreshing at roughly half that granted\nvalue, a conventional TURN client practice. Both the allocation itself\n(refreshed via a real signed STUN Refresh request, reusing\n`turn_relay.cljc`'s existing message-construction helpers, extended for\nthis new request type) and the peer permission (independently\nshorter-lived per RFC 8656 §9, refreshed via a fresh CreatePermission call\non the same schedule) are kept alive. A single failed refresh attempt is\nlogged and retried on the next interval rather than crashing the node —\nexplicitly scoped as \"no exponential backoff, no give-up-after-N-failures\"\nin the docstring, a production-hardening increment left for later, not\nsilently assumed solved.\n\n### Decision 2: an asymmetry in `org-ietf-turn`'s own listener discovered while designing the test — recorded, not worked around invisibly\n\nReading `kotoba.turn.listener`'s source directly (not assumed): the\nAllocate handler ignores any client-requested LIFETIME on the *original*\nAllocate request entirely, always granting the fixed 600s default. The\nRefresh handler, in contrast, genuinely honors a client-requested\nLIFETIME. This asymmetry meant Scenario 4 (below) could not shrink the\nliteral initial grant to make a realistic-length automated test window —\nso it uses the Refresh path (the exact feature under test, not a\nworkaround around it) to bring a real, live allocation down to a short,\nserver-granted 3-second window on its first scheduled tick, then proves\ncontinued scheduling keeps it alive past that real deadline. This is\nSTRONGER than an artificial short-Allocate-window test would have been:\nit exercises the actual refresh mechanism a production node would use,\nnot a special-cased short-lived-Allocate-only test path.\n\n### Decision 3: a genuine negative control, not merely \"no exception was thrown\"\n\nDemo Scenario 4b runs the identical short-window scenario with refresh\nscheduling disabled after the same manual shrink-to-3-seconds, waits the\nsame real 5.5 seconds, and confirms the SERVER's own expiry sweep drops\nthe allocation (`org-ietf-turn`'s own listener logs `TURN-LISTENER expiry\nsweep dropped 1 allocation(s)` — the server's log, not the client's) and\nthat a subsequent send genuinely fails to arrive. This directly answers\nthe question a skeptical reader of Scenario 4a's \"success\" would ask —\n*would that send have worked anyway, refresh or not?* — with a real,\nindependently-observable counterfactual failure, not an assumption.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `clojure -M:test` → 45 tests / 226 assertions (was 212; +14 new for `turn_relay.cljc`'s `build-refresh-request`/`response-lifetime`), 0 failures, 0 errors. Independently re-run from a cold clone.\n- `clojure -M:lint` → 0 errors, 0 warnings. Independently re-run.\n- `nbb --classpath \"src:test:../phone/src:../html/src:../css/src:../wire/src:../bytes/src:../org-ietf-turn/src\" test/kotoba/dtn/transport/udp_turn_demo.cljs` → `RESULT: 4/4 scenarios passed`, exit 0. Scenarios 1-3 (from ADR-2607162304) unmodified and still passing. New Scenario 4a: a real `js/setTimeout`-based 5.5-second wait, no mocked clock, past a real 3-second server-granted window, with a message genuinely still delivered afterward. New Scenario 4b: the same window with refresh disabled, the server's own log confirming a genuine expiry sweep, and the equivalent send genuinely failing to arrive. Independently re-run from a cold clone by the orchestrating session — output identical to the implementing agent's report, including the server-side `TURN-LISTENER expiry sweep dropped 1 allocation(s)` line.\n- `dtn`'s other two demos (7-scenario TCP transport, 3-scenario discovery) independently re-run and confirmed unaffected.\n- `org-ietf-turn` confirmed completely untouched (`git status --short` empty on a fresh independent clone) — this ADR's design relied on reading, not modifying, its listener's actual behavior (Decision 2).\n- Pushed to `github.com/kotoba-lang/dtn` (public), commit `2235e2fb2d8d12c27a37c8be4e3b85b3d0fa4bc3`.\n- `manifest/west.yml`: pin advanced via `--entry dtn` (1-line diff, `838910679487→2235e2fb2d8d`, `verify-west-pins: 1 件の pin 変更をすべて検証 OK`), landed via an isolated sibling-path worktree + GitHub API server-side merge, matching every prior ADR in this series.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- The specific, named limitation from ADR-2607162304's own Consequences\n  (\"a relay allocated at startup will silently expire... a real limitation\n  for any long-running deployment\") is closed — a `:turn-relay`-configured\n  `dtn` node now stays reachable for as long as the process runs, not just\n  for the first ~5-10 minutes.\n- The verification method for this fix (Decision 2/3) is itself a small\n  methodological point worth carrying forward: when a server's behavior is\n  uncertain, read the actual source rather than assuming RFC defaults\n  hold, and when proving \"X prevents Y,\" include a negative control that\n  demonstrates Y actually happens without X — not just that X doesn't\n  crash.\n- Still explicitly scoped down, unchanged from ADR-2607162304's own\n  disclosed list plus this ADR's own Decision 1 addition: no exponential\n  backoff/give-up logic on repeated refresh failure, no dynamic\n  cadence adjustment if a server ever granted an unusually short initial\n  lifetime (this ADR's fixed half-of-granted-lifetime cadence assumes the\n  initial grant is reasonably long, which held for `org-ietf-turn`'s fixed\n  600s default per Decision 2 — a server granting, say, a 2-second initial\n  lifetime would need a correspondingly faster cadence than \"half of 2\n  seconds\" might comfortably allow for network round-trip time; not a\n  concern for the server this was verified against, but named as a\n  boundary condition future work should be aware of).\n- This is likely the last item this ADR series will close under its own\n  originating scope — remaining possibilities (full ICE, IPv6, long-term\n  credentials, DoS/quota limits, production retry/backoff) are genuine\n  production-hardening increments, not gaps in what this series set out to\n  prove (\"is an independent, wire-compatible telecom substrate actually\n  operational\"), and would be a new, separately-scoped decision if pursued.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- **Shrinking the original Allocate request's LIFETIME to make Scenario 4\n  testable in short real time**, instead of using the Refresh path.\n  Rejected once Decision 2's finding (the listener ignores a\n  client-requested LIFETIME on Allocate) was made — not a design choice so\n  much as a discovered constraint, but recorded because the resulting test\n  design (exercising Refresh, the actual feature under test, rather than a\n  hypothetical short-Allocate path) turned out to be the stronger proof\n  anyway.\n- **A vacuous Scenario 4** (just wait and confirm no exception) instead of\n  a real negative control. Rejected: this whole ADR series has\n  consistently valued non-vacuous proofs (ADR-2607162202's Decision 4 made\n  the identical point about a mesh topology's own transport-layer\n  exclusion making a naive self-filtering check vacuous) — the 5.5-second\n  wait's significance is only provable by showing the counterfactual\n  genuinely fails, which Scenario 4b does.\n- **Full production retry/backoff on refresh failure** as part of this\n  pass. Rejected for now: log-and-retry-next-interval is sufficient to\n  close the specific named gap (silent permanent expiry); backoff/give-up\n  logic is a real but separable hardening increment, named explicitly as\n  still open rather than bundled in partially-done.\n\n## References\n\n- `90-docs/adr/2607162304-kotoba-lang-dtn-udp-turn-nat-traversal.edn` (where this gap was named)\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn/README.edn` — https://github.com/kotoba-lang/dtn\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn/src/kotoba/dtn/transport/udp.cljs`\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn/test/kotoba/dtn/transport/udp_turn_demo.cljs`\n"), (:adr/artifacts "{:kotoba-lang-dtn \"kotoba-lang/dtn @ 2235e2fb2d8d (pushed, public)\", :manifest \"orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn pin advanced via --entry dtn, pin-verified\"}"), (:db/id -1), (:adr/authoritative true), (:adr/title "ADR-2607162331: kotoba-lang/dtn gains automatic TURN allocation/permission refresh — a :turn-relay node stays reachable indefinitely instead of silently expiring"), (:adr/source-format "edn")))